(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-7-pulmonary-vascular-disorders-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Pulmonary Vascular Disorders Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 7","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 7","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Over what time course does acute cor pulmonale develop?","choices":["Minutes to hours","Days to weeks","Weeks to months","Months to years"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Acute cor pulmonale, typically from a massive pulmonary embolism, develops over minutes to hours with sudden right ventricular dilation and dysfunction. It presents with severe sudden dyspnea, sweating, rapid pulse, pallor and syncope, and it threatens immediate cardiovascular collapse. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which heart failure stage mandates immediate evaluation for advanced therapies such as heart transplant?","choices":["Stage A","Stage B","Stage C","Stage D"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Stage D, the break point, means marked symptoms at rest despite maximal medical therapy and frequent hospitalizations. Reaching it mandates immediate evaluation for advanced therapies: heart transplant, mechanical circulatory support or palliative hospice care. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which hemodynamic pattern fits cor pulmonale?","choices":["High pulmonary artery pressure, normal wedge","High pulmonary artery pressure, high wedge","Normal pulmonary artery pressure, high wedge","Normal pulmonary artery pressure, low wedge"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cor pulmonale combines an elevated mean pulmonary artery pressure with a normal pulmonary capillary wedge pressure, because the problem lies in the lungs or pulmonary vessels rather than the left heart. A wedge at or below 15 mmHg with a raised mean pulmonary artery pressure is the right-sided pattern. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 4, 23"},{"q":"During flash pulmonary edema with acute kidney injury, what is done with outpatient heart failure therapy?","choices":["Continue it unchanged","Intensify it","Stop it permanently","Hold it temporarily"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Outpatient therapy is temporarily held during acute kidney injury while the acute episode is treated with upright positioning, oxygen or noninvasive ventilation, intravenous furosemide and intravenous nitrates. Oral beta-blockers are the exception, continued unless the patient is in shock. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 45, 46"},{"q":"Which test is essential to exclude chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension?","choices":["Echocardiography","Right heart catheterization","Ventilation-perfusion scan","Computed tomographic angiography"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The ventilation-perfusion scan is the essential test to exclude chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension. Computed tomographic angiography should not be relied on alone, because a high-probability ventilation-perfusion mismatch sends the patient down a completely different, surgical pathway (thromboendarterectomy). — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"In healthy lungs, what does localized hypoxic vasoconstriction achieve?","choices":["Raising blood viscosity","Better ventilation-perfusion matching","Higher right ventricular afterload","Diverting blood to hypoxic areas"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In healthy lungs, localized hypoxia triggers focused vasoconstriction that shunts blood to better-oxygenated areas, optimizing the ventilation-perfusion ratio. It is a protective, targeted reflex; it turns harmful when the hypoxia is global, as in chronic lung disease. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 6, 7"},{"q":"A patient has a heart rate above 100, hemoptysis and surgery 10 days earlier; pulmonary embolism is not the leading diagnosis, and there are no leg signs, prior clots or cancer. What is the Wells score?","choices":["2.5 points","3.0 points","4.0 points","5.5 points"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Heart rate above 100 earns 1.5, surgery in the previous 4 weeks earns 1.5 and hemoptysis earns 1.0: 1.5 + 1.5 + 1.0 = 4.0. Embolism is not the leading diagnosis (no 3.0), and there are no signs of deep venous thrombosis, prior clots or malignancy. A total of 4 or less means embolism is unlikely. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"What does pink, frothy sputum show in pulmonary edema?","choices":["Distal pulmonary infarction","Interstitial septal engorgement","Bronchospasm from inflammation","Profound alveolar fluid shift"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pink, frothy sputum is the visual proof of a profound alveolar fluid shift with microvascular hemorrhage. With bilateral crackles and wheezing, an S3 gallop and acute dyspnea with diaphoresis and tripoding, it marks a flooded lung. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 35, 37, 43, 50"},{"q":"What does the S1Q3T3 pattern on the electrocardiogram suggest?","choices":["Severe right ventricular strain","Pulmonary embolism specifically","Left ventricular hypertrophy","Right atrial enlargement"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The S1Q3T3 pattern, a deep S wave in lead I with a Q wave and an inverted T wave in lead III, suggests severe right ventricular strain, not purely pulmonary embolism. It is highly specific but has low sensitivity, so its absence does not exclude embolism. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 51, 14"},{"q":"In the stepwise workup of cor pulmonale, what is the ventilation-perfusion scan used for?","choices":["To confirm obstructive lung disease","To measure the wedge pressure","To estimate pulmonary artery pressure","To exclude thromboembolic disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. After the chest film and pulmonary function tests, step 2 is a ventilation-perfusion scan or computed tomographic pulmonary angiography to exclude segmental defects from chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension or pulmonary embolism (group 4). The ventilation-perfusion scan is the essential test to exclude chronic thromboembolic disease. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 12, 13, 14, 24"},{"q":"Which risk factor belongs to the endothelial injury arm of Virchow's triad?","choices":["Prolonged bed rest","Genetic thrombophilia","Central venous catheters","Estrogen therapy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Endothelial injury includes recent surgery, central venous catheters, pacemaker wires and pelvic or lower limb trauma. Hypercoagulability and venous stasis are the other two arms of Virchow's triad, the origins of thrombosis. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which World Health Organization pulmonary hypertension group includes connective tissue disease, human immunodeficiency virus infection and schistosomiasis?","choices":["Group 1","Group 2","Group 3","Group 5"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Group 1, pulmonary arterial hypertension, is pre-capillary disease with an obliterative and proliferative mechanism. Its causes are idiopathic disease, connective tissue disease, human immunodeficiency virus infection and schistosomiasis. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Sildenafil and tadalafil belong to which pulmonary arterial hypertension drug class?","choices":["Endothelin receptor antagonists","Prostacyclin receptor agonists","Calcium channel blockers","Phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sildenafil and tadalafil are phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitors, one of the specialized targeted therapies for pulmonary arterial hypertension (group 1) alongside endothelin receptor antagonists and prostacyclins. In low or intermediate risk disease, one is paired with an endothelin receptor antagonist as oral combination therapy. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 15, 26, 28"},{"q":"Which cause of pulmonary edema is noncardiogenic?","choices":["Inhaled chemical toxins","Ischemic myocardial loss","Chronic aortic regurgitation","Age-related myocardial stiffening"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Inhaled chemical injury to the alveolar-capillary membrane is a noncardiogenic root of pulmonary edema, along with acute respiratory distress syndrome (severe inflammatory capillary destruction) and sepsis (systemic vasodilation and endothelial leak). Each damages the barrier rather than raising pressure. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which systemic finding reflects the venous backlog of chronic cor pulmonale?","choices":["Bibasilar crackles","Pink frothy sputum","Kerley B lines","Tender hepatomegaly"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. When the hypertrophied right ventricle decompensates, elevated diastolic pressures transmit backward into the systemic venous circulation and raise hydrostatic pressure. The systemic congestion shows as tender hepatomegaly (hepatic congestion), ascites and dependent peripheral pitting edema. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 11, 23, 35, 37"},{"q":"Which pulmonary embolism favors indefinite anticoagulation?","choices":["One with active malignancy","One after recent surgery","One during estrogen therapy","One after trauma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. At the 3-month decision point, a persistent or unprovoked risk (active malignancy, a hypercoagulable state or no identifiable risk factor) favors indefinite anticoagulation to prevent recurrence. Transient, provoked risks allow stopping to be considered after 3 months. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Besides vasoconstriction, how do emphysema, fibrosis and pulmonary embolism raise pulmonary vascular resistance?","choices":["Loss of vascular cross-sectional area","Rising plasma oncotic pressure","Increased capillary permeability","Obstructed pulmonary venous outflow"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In emphysema, interstitial fibrosis and pulmonary embolism the rise in resistance is anatomical as well as functional: structural effacement severely reduces the total cross-sectional area of the pulmonary vascular bed. Polycythemia from chronic hypoxia compounds it by raising blood viscosity. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 7, 19, 32, 33"},{"q":"In pulmonary hypertension, what does syncope or presyncope signify?","choices":["A rising cardiac output","A fixed, low cardiac output","Early, mild disease","Left ventricular systolic failure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Syncope or presyncope is an ominous sign of a fixed, low cardiac output that cannot increase with exertion. Septal bowing from the overloaded right ventricle impairs left ventricular filling, and repeated syncope places a patient in the high-risk prognostic group. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 20, 21, 25"},{"q":"What is the most useful initial test when pulmonary hypertension is suspected?","choices":["Right heart catheterization","Ventilation-perfusion scan","Computed tomographic angiography","Echocardiography"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Echocardiography is the screening test and the most useful initial test: it estimates right ventricular size and function and pulmonary pressures, and it identifies left-sided valvular or myocardial disease that can mimic pulmonary hypertension. Right heart catheterization then confirms the diagnosis. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"For which pulmonary embolism risk category is thrombolysis highly controversial?","choices":["Chronic thromboembolic disease","Submassive (intermediate risk)","Massive (high risk)","Low risk, normotensive"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Systemic and catheter-directed thrombolysis is the gold standard for massive, hemodynamically unstable embolism, but it is highly controversial in submassive embolism, where rapid right ventricular recovery must be weighed against major bleeding and intracranial hemorrhage risk. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 58, 60, 61"},{"q":"A patient with risk factors for pulmonary embolism has no shortness of breath. How should that absence be weighed?","choices":["It lowers the Wells score","Do not rely on it","It rules out embolism","It excludes a massive clot"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Do not rely on shortness of breath: approximately 25% of patients with proven pulmonary embolism completely lack dyspnea. Its absence therefore cannot lower suspicion; the workup still runs through the Wells score and, when embolism is unlikely, the rule-out criteria and D-dimer. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 50, 53, 54, 58"},{"q":"Cor pulmonale is a change in right ventricular structure and function caused exclusively by which kind of disorder?","choices":["Left-sided valvular heart disease","Primary lung or pulmonary vessel disease","Left ventricular systolic dysfunction","Elevated left-sided filling pressures"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cor pulmonale is an alteration in the structure and function of the right ventricle (enlargement, hypertrophy or dilation) caused exclusively by a primary disorder of the respiratory system or the pulmonary vasculature. Its hemodynamic signature is an elevated mean pulmonary artery pressure with a normal pulmonary capillary wedge pressure. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 4, 19"},{"q":"Why are early palliative care discussions and advance directives essential in pulmonary arterial hypertension?","choices":["Progression is slow and predictable","Sudden death may be the first sign","Cardiac arrest is usually survivable","The right ventricle recovers after arrest"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sudden death or lethal syncope can be the first indication of disease progression, so early palliative care discussions and advance directives are essential. Survival at 90 days after cardiopulmonary resuscitation in pulmonary arterial hypertension is 6%, because the hypertrophied, failed right ventricle rarely recovers from arrest. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What is first-line imaging for almost all patients with suspected pulmonary embolism?","choices":["Ventilation-perfusion lung scanning","Bedside transthoracic echocardiography","Conventional catheter pulmonary angiography","Computed tomographic pulmonary angiography"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Computed tomographic pulmonary angiography is first-line imaging for almost all patients with suspected embolism. Its limitations are intravenous iodinated contrast, radiation exposure and the risk of overdiagnosing clinically insignificant subsegmental emboli. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 55, 56, 61, 62"},{"q":"When is the PERC (Pulmonary Embolism Rule-out Criteria) applied in the pulmonary embolism workup?","choices":["After a positive D-dimer","Instead of the Wells score","After a Wells score of 4 or less","After a Wells score above 4"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The Wells score is calculated first. When it is 4 or less (embolism unlikely), the PERC (Pulmonary Embolism Rule-out Criteria) are applied for very low clinical gestalt: if all eight are true the workup stops; if any fails, a D-dimer follows. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 54, 57, 62"},{"q":"What do global alveolar hypoxia and hypercapnia in chronic lung disease cause in the pulmonary circulation?","choices":["Diffuse vasodilation","Capillary membrane leak","Diffuse vasoconstriction","Focused vasoconstriction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In chronic lung disease, especially the blue bloater with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, global alveolar hypoxia and hypercapnia trigger diffuse, system-wide vasoconstriction. The result is a massive increase in pulmonary vascular resistance and a relentless rise in right ventricular afterload. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 6, 15, 27, 33"},{"q":"Above which pulmonary capillary wedge pressure is pulmonary edema confirmed as cardiogenic?","choices":["Above 5 mmHg","Above 10 mmHg","Above 18 to 20 mmHg","Above 30 mmHg"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A pulmonary capillary wedge pressure above 18 to 20 mmHg confirms cardiogenic, hydrostatic failure: intact capillaries under extreme hydrostatic backup from a failing pump. Noncardiogenic edema, a permeability problem, keeps the wedge at or below 18 mmHg. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 33, 38"},{"q":"Which finding distinguishes a chronic obstructive pulmonary disease exacerbation from cardiogenic pulmonary edema?","choices":["Dyspnea","Hypercapnia","Hypoxemia","Tachycardia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Dyspnea, hypoxemia and tachycardia are shared by heart failure, pulmonary embolism and a chronic obstructive pulmonary disease exacerbation. The exacerbation is distinguished by wheezing or hyperinflation on the film, hypercapnia and a usually normal natriuretic peptide. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which finding points to right heart failure from cor pulmonale rather than left heart failure?","choices":["Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea","Kerley B lines on film","Lungs clear to auscultation","Bibasilar crackles"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In right heart failure from cor pulmonale the lungs are typically clear to auscultation, and the chest film shows enlarged central pulmonary arteries with peripheral pruning; the wedge pressure is normal or low (at or below 15 mmHg). Abdominal bloating, early satiety, profound fatigue and exertional syncope dominate. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which pulmonary function result places interstitial lung disease among the causes of group 3 pulmonary hypertension?","choices":["Flattened diaphragms on film","Reduced total lung capacity","Reduced expiratory flow ratio","Increased total lung capacity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A restrictive pattern, reduced total lung capacity, identifies interstitial lung disease and is crucial for classifying group 3 pulmonary hypertension. The obstructive pattern of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease instead shows a reduced ratio of forced expiratory volume in one second to forced vital capacity. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 12, 13"}]};
